pg_bp_rcrd -- RCRD (Recent Consecutive Reuse Distance) Buffer Pool Strategy
============================================================================

This extension provides the RCRD algorithm (based on LIRS2) as a
buffer pool replacement strategy for PostgreSQL's multi-buffer-pool
infrastructure.

RCRD tracks the Consecutive Reuse Distance (CRD) of each cached page --
the number of distinct pages accessed between consecutive accesses to
the same page.  Pages with small CRD are classified as "hot" and kept
in the cache; pages with large CRD are "cold" and eligible for eviction.

When to Use RCRD
----------------

RCRD is well-suited for:
  - Mixed OLTP/analytical workloads with varying access patterns
  - Workloads with scan-heavy access that should not pollute the hot set
  - Situations where ARC or CAR over-react to frequency changes
  - Workloads where LIRS performs well but you want lower overhead

RCRD vs LIRS
-------------

RCRD uses CRD (distance-based) classification instead of LIRS's
stack-position-based IRR tracking.  This results in:
  - Simpler internal bookkeeping (no stack pruning invariant)
  - Adaptive hot threshold that grows on ghost hits
  - Similar or better hit rates on scan-heavy workloads

Usage
-----

  -- Load the extension (requires shared_preload_libraries for DEFAULT pool)
  CREATE EXTENSION pg_bp_rcrd;

  -- Create a dedicated RCRD buffer pool
  CREATE BUFFER POOL my_rcrd_pool HANDLER rcrd_pool_handler SIZE '64MB';

  -- Assign tables to the pool
  CREATE TABLE hot_data (...) WITH (buffer_pool = 'my_rcrd_pool');

  -- Monitor statistics
  SELECT * FROM pg_stat_rcrd;

  -- Get sizing advice
  SELECT * FROM pg_bp_rcrd_size_recommendation('my_rcrd_pool')
      AS (current_size int, recommended_size int,
          ghost_pressure float8, hit_ratio float8);

Statistics
----------

The pg_stat_rcrd view provides per-pool statistics:
  - hot_size, cold_size, ghost_size: current page counts
  - hot_capacity: adaptive threshold for hot classification
  - r_size: recency list size
  - q_size: cold list size
  - lookups, hot_hits, cold_hits, ghost_hits, misses
  - demotions, promotions, evictions, threshold_raises

VACUUM Integration
------------------

When VACUUM is active, newly accessed pages are inserted at the Q head
(eviction end) for fast eviction, preventing VACUUM scans from polluting
the cache.

Limitations
-----------

  - CRD computation traverses the recency list, which is O(n) in the
    worst case.  In practice, hot pages have small CRD and the traversal
    is short.
  - The hot_capacity threshold only grows (on ghost hits); it does not
    shrink.  This is intentional for stability but means the algorithm
    may over-allocate hot slots after a workload change.

References
----------

  C. Zhong, X. Zhao, and S. Jiang, "LIRS2: An Improved LIRS Replacement
  Algorithm", Technical Report, 2013.
